description:
pkix-ct/src/verify.rs:697-705

```rust
#[must_use]
pub fn merkle_leaf_hash(leaf_bytes: &[u8]) -> [u8; 32] {
    let mut h = Sha256::new();
    h.update([0x00u8]);
    h.update(leaf_bytes);
    ...
}
```

The signature does not constrain what `leaf_bytes` is. The doc says
it's `MerkleTreeLeaf` bytes (built by `merkle_tree_leaf_for_cert` or
`merkle_tree_leaf_for_precert`), but at the type level any byte
sequence is acceptable.

A caller using the type system to track wire-format identity will be
disappointed. A typo passing the SCT signature bytes instead of the
MerkleTreeLeaf bytes will silently produce a wrong hash with no
diagnostic.

Recommendation: wrap the byte sequence in a newtype.

```rust
pub struct MerkleTreeLeafBytes(Vec<u8>);

impl MerkleTreeLeafBytes {
    pub fn for_cert(sct: &SCT, cert_der: &[u8]) -> Result<Self> {
        ... // current merkle_tree_leaf_for_cert
    }
    pub fn for_precert(sct: &SCT, leaf: &[u8], issuer: &[u8]) -> Result<Self> { ... }
    pub fn hash(&self) -> [u8; 32] { merkle_leaf_hash(&self.0) }
    pub fn as_bytes(&self) -> &[u8] { &self.0 }
}
```

Then `merkle_leaf_hash(&[u8])` becomes private; the public path is
`MerkleTreeLeafBytes::for_cert(...).hash()`. Cleaner contract and
caller cannot pass wrong bytes.

Cost: an extra newtype in the public surface, and a small allocation
the caller has to manage. Benefit: the type system enforces that
what you hash is what you built.

Priority P3 because the existing API works for correct callers; the
risk is to careless callers, and the test fixture audit at the
oracle level catches it for the workspace's own tests.
